</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="38" id="mwCSo"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Negative_side_effects">Negative side effects</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1131" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Essay-like","href":"./Template:Essay-like"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"May 2026"},"1":{"wt":"section"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCSs"><style data-mw-deduplicate="TemplateStyles:r1384556748" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" about="#mwt1132" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Message box/ambox.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'>.mw-parser-output .ambox{border:1px solid var(--border-color-base,#a2a9b1);border-left:10px solid #36c;background-color:var(--background-color-neutral-subtle,#f8f9fa);box-sizing:border-box}.mw-parser-output .ambox+.mw-empty-elt+.ambox,.mw-parser-output .ambox+.mw-empty-elt+.mw-empty-elt+.ambox,.mw-parser-output .ambox+.mw-empty-elt+.mw-empty-elt+.mw-empty-elt+.ambox{margin-top:-1px}html body.mediawiki .mw-parser-output .ambox.mbox-small-left{margin:4px 1em 4px 0;overflow:hidden;width:238px;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:88%;line-height:1.25em}.mw-parser-output .ambox-speedy{border-left-color:#b32424;background-color:#fee7e6}.mw-parser-output .ambox-delete{border-left-color:#b32424}.mw-parser-output .ambox-content{border-left-color:#f28500}.mw-parser-output .ambox-style{border-left-color:#fc3}.mw-parser-output .ambox-move{border-left-color:#9932cc}.mw-parser-output .ambox-protection{border-left-color:var(--border-color-base,#a2a9b1)}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-text{border:none;padding:0.25em 0.5em;width:100%}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-image{border:none;padding:2px 0 2px 0.5em;text-align:center}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-imageright{border:none;padding:2px 0.5em 2px 0;text-align:center}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-empty-cell{border:none;padding:0;width:1px}.mw-parser-output .ambox .mbox-image-div{width:52px}@media(min-width:720px){.mw-parser-output .ambox{margin:0 10%}}@media print{body.ns-0 .mw-parser-output .ambox{display:none!important}}@media screen{html.skin-theme-clientpref-night .mw-parser-output .ambox-speedy{background-color:#310402}}@media screen and (prefers-color-scheme:dark){html.skin-theme-clientpref-os .mw-parser-output .ambox-speedy{background-color:#310402}}</style></span><table class="box-Essay-like plainlinks metadata ambox ambox-style ambox-essay-like" role="presentation" about="#mwt1131"><tbody><tr><td class="mbox-image"><div class="mbox-image-div"><span typeof="mw:File"><span><img resource="./File:Edit-clear.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f2/Edit-clear.svg/40px-Edit-clear.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/f/f2/Edit-clear.svg/120px-Edit-clear.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" alt="" data-file-width="48" data-file-height="48" data-file-type="drawing" height="40" width="40" class="mw-file-element"/></span></span></div></td><td class="mbox-text"><div class="mbox-text-span">This section <b>is written like a <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wikipedia:What_Wikipedia_is_not#Wikipedia_is_not_a_publisher_of_original_thought" title="Wikipedia:What Wikipedia is not">personal reflection, personal essay, or argumentative essay</a></b> that states a Wikipedia editor's personal feelings or presents an original argument about a topic.<span class="hide-when-compact"> Please <a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Climate_change_mitigation&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Climate_change_mitigation&amp;action=edit">help improve it</a> by rewriting it in an <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wikipedia:Writing_better_articles#Information_style_and_tone" title="Wikipedia:Writing better articles">encyclopedic style</a>.</span>  <span class="date-container"><i>(<span class="date">May 2026</span>)</i></span><span class="hide-when-compact"><i> (<small><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Help:Maintenance_template_removal" title="Help:Maintenance template removal">Learn how and when to remove this message</a></small>)</i></span></div></td></tr></tbody></table><span typeof="mw:Nowiki" about="#mwt1131"></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Wikipedia_articles_with_style_issues_from_May_2026" about="#mwt1131"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_articles_with_style_issues" about="#mwt1131" id="mwCSw"/>
<p id="mwCS0">Mitigation measures can also have negative side effects and risks.<sup about="#mwt1135" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-IPCC-2022_100-8" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IPCC-2022"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-IPCC-2022-100" id="mwCS4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCS8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTA">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1133" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"TS-133"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCTI"><span title="Page / location: TS-133"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>TS-133<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In agriculture and forestry, mitigation measures can affect biodiversity and ecosystem functioning.<sup about="#mwt1136" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-IPCC-2022_100-9" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IPCC-2022"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-IPCC-2022-100" id="mwCTM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCTQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTU">[</span>100<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTY">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1134" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"TS-87"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCTc"><span title="Page / location: TS-87"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>TS-87<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> In renewable energy, mining for metals and minerals can increase threats to conservation areas.<sup about="#mwt1139" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-305" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-305"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-305" id="mwCTg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCTk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTo">[</span>305<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCTs">]</span></span></a></sup> There is some research into ways to recycle solar panels and electronic waste. This would create a source for materials so there is no need to mine them.<sup about="#mwt1142" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-techrev_306-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"techrev"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-techrev-306"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-techrev-306" id="mwCTw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCT0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCT4">[</span>306<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCT8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1145" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Lamb_et_al._307-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Lamb et al."},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Lamb_et_al.-307"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Lamb_et_al.-307" id="mwCUA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCUE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUI">[</span>307<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUM">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p id="mwCUQ">Scholars have found that discussions about risks and negative side effects of mitigation measures can lead to deadlock or the feeling that there are insuperable barriers to taking action.<sup about="#mwt1146" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Lamb_et_al._307-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Lamb et al."}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Lamb_et_al.-307" id="mwCUU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCUY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUc">[</span>307<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUg">]</span></span></a></sup> This deadlock or feelings often happens because people focus more on possible losses than benefits, making them hesitant to support change.<sup about="#mwt1149" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-308" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-308"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-308" id="mwCUk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCUo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUs">[</span>308<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCUw">]</span></span></a></sup> Uncertainty also plays a role, as decision-makers may overthink worst-case scenarios and delay action instead of moving forward.<sup about="#mwt1152" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-309" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-309"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-309" id="mwCU0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCU4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCU8">[</span>309<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVA">]</span></span></a></sup> In addition, strong disagreements between groups and the lack of a shared way to judge risks can make compromise difficult, leading to political gridlock.<sup about="#mwt1155" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-310" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-310"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-310" id="mwCVE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCVI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVM">[</span>310<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVQ">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="39" id="mwCVU"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Costs_and_funding">Costs and funding</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1156" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Economics of climate change mitigation#Assessing costs and benefits"},"2":{"wt":"Economic analysis of climate change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCVY"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1157" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1156" id="mwCVc">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Economics_of_climate_change_mitigation#Assessing_costs_and_benefits" title="Economics of climate change mitigation" class="mw-redirect">Economics of climate change mitigation §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Assessing costs and benefits</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Economic_analysis_of_climate_change" title="Economic analysis of climate change">Economic analysis of climate change</a></div>

<p id="mwCVg">Several factors affect mitigation cost estimates. One is the baseline. This is a reference scenario that the alternative mitigation scenario is compared with. Others are the way costs are modelled, and assumptions about future government policy.<sup about="#mwt1162" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-311" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-311"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-311" id="mwCVk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCVo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVs">[</span>311<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCVw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1158" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"622"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCV0"><span title="Page / location: 622"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>622<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Cost estimates for mitigation for specific regions depend on the quantity of emissions allowed for that region in future, as well as the timing of interventions.<sup about="#mwt1164" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-312" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-312"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-312" id="mwCV4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCV8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWA">[</span>312<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWE">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1159" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"90"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCWI"><span title="Page / location: 90"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>90<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwCWM">Mitigation costs will vary according to how and when emissions are cut. Early, well-planned action will minimise the costs.<sup about="#mwt1165" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-stern_156-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"stern"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-stern-156" id="mwCWQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCWU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWY">[</span>156<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWc">]</span></span></a></sup> Globally, the benefits of keeping warming under 2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCWg"> </span>°C exceed the costs,<sup about="#mwt1168" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sampedro-2020_313-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sampedro-2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sampedro-2020-313"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Sampedro-2020-313" id="mwCWk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCWo"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWs">[</span>313<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCWw">]</span></span></a></sup> which according to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./The_Economist" title="The Economist" id="mwCW0">The Economist</a> are affordable.<sup about="#mwt1171" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-314" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-314"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-314" id="mwCW4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCW8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXA">[</span>314<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCXI">Economists estimate the cost of climate change mitigation at between 1% and 2% of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Gross_domestic_product" title="Gross domestic product" id="mwCXM">GDP</a>.<sup about="#mwt1174" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Rhys-2019_315-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Rhys-2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Rhys-2019-315"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Rhys-2019-315" id="mwCXQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCXU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXY">[</span>315<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1180" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Kotz2024_316-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Kotz2024"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Kotz2024-316"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Kotz2024-316" id="mwCXg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCXk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXo">[</span>316<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCXs">]</span></span></a></sup> While this is a large sum, it is still far less than the subsidies governments provide to the fossil fuel industry. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./International_Monetary_Fund" title="International Monetary Fund" id="mwCXw">International Monetary Fund</a> estimated this at more than $5 trillion per year.<sup about="#mwt1183" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-317" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-317"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-317" id="mwCX0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCX4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCX8">[</span>317<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1186" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Teske-2019_50-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Teske-2019"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Teske-2019-50"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Teske-2019-50" id="mwCYE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYM">[</span>50<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYQ">]</span></span></a></sup> A 2024 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Opinion_poll" title="Opinion poll" id="mwCYU">opinion poll</a> found 69% of the global population were willing to volunteer 1% of their income for climate change mitigation.<sup about="#mwt1189" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-r865_318-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"r865"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-r865-318"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-r865-318" id="mwCYY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYg">[</span>318<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCYk">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCYo">Another estimate says that financial flows for climate mitigation and adaptation are going to be over $800 billion per year. These financial requirements are predicted to exceed $4 trillion per year by 2030.<sup about="#mwt1192" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-319" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-319"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-319" id="mwCYs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCYw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCY0">[</span>319<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCY4">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1195" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-320" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-320"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-320" id="mwCY8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZE">[</span>320<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZI">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCZM">Globally, limiting warming to 2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCZQ"> </span>°C may result in higher economic benefits than economic costs.<sup about="#mwt1199" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-321" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-321"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-321" id="mwCZU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZc">[</span>321<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1196" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"300"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCZk"><span title="Page / location: 300"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>300<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The economic repercussions of mitigation vary widely across regions and households, depending on policy design and level of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./International_cooperation" title="International cooperation" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCZo">international cooperation</a>. Delayed global cooperation increases policy costs across regions, especially in those that are relatively carbon intensive at present. Pathways with uniform carbon values show higher mitigation costs in more carbon-intensive regions, in fossil-fuels exporting regions and in poorer regions. Aggregate quantifications expressed in GDP or monetary terms undervalue the economic effects on households in poorer countries. The actual effects on welfare and well-being are comparatively larger.<sup about="#mwt1200" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_3_322-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 3"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_3-322"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_3-322" id="mwCZs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCZw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZ0">[</span>322<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCZ4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCZ8"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Cost–benefit_analysis" title="Cost–benefit analysis" id="mwCaA">Cost–benefit analysis</a> may be unsuitable for analysing climate change mitigation as a whole. But it is still useful for analysing the difference between a 1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCaE"> </span>°C target and 2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCaI"> </span>°C.<sup about="#mwt1201" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Rhys-2019_315-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Rhys-2019"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Rhys-2019-315" id="mwCaM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCaQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCaU">[</span>315<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCaY">]</span></span></a></sup> One way of estimating the cost of reducing emissions is by considering the likely costs of potential technological and output changes. Policymakers can compare the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Marginal_abatement_costs" title="Marginal abatement costs" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCac">marginal abatement costs</a> of different methods to assess the cost and amount of possible abatement over time. The marginal abatement costs of the various measures will differ by country, by sector, and over time.<sup about="#mwt1202" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-stern_156-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"stern"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-stern-156" id="mwCag"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCak"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCao">[</span>156<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCas">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCaw"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Eco-tariff" title="Eco-tariff" id="mwCa0">Eco-tariffs</a> on only imports contribute to reduced global export <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Competition_(economics)" title="Competition (economics)" id="mwCa4">competitiveness</a> and to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Deindustrialisation" title="Deindustrialisation" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCa8">deindustrialisation</a>.<sup about="#mwt1205" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-o344_323-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"o344"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-o344-323"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-o344-323" id="mwCbA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCbE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbI">[</span>323<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="40" id="mwCbQ"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Avoided_costs_of_climate_change_effects">Avoided costs of climate change effects</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1206" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Economic impacts of climate change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCbU"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1207" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1206" id="mwCbY">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Economic_impacts_of_climate_change" title="Economic impacts of climate change" class="mw-redirect">Economic impacts of climate change</a></div>

<p id="mwCbc">It is possible to avoid some of the costs of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Effects_of_climate_change" title="Effects of climate change" id="mwCbg">effects of climate change</a> by limiting climate change. According to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Stern_Review" title="Stern Review" id="mwCbk">Stern Review</a>, inaction can be as high as the equivalent of losing at least 5% of global gross domestic product (GDP) each year, now and forever. This can be up to 20% of GDP or more when including a wider range of risks and impacts. But mitigating climate change will only cost about 2% of GDP. Also it may not be a good idea from a financial perspective to delay significant reductions in greenhouse gas emissions.<sup about="#mwt1210" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-324" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-324"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-324" id="mwCbo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCbs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCbw">[</span>324<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCb0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1213" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-325" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-325"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-325" id="mwCb4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCb8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcA">[</span>325<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCcI">Mitigation solutions are often evaluated in terms of costs and greenhouse gas reduction potentials. This fails to take into account the direct effects on human well-being.<sup about="#mwt1216" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-326" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-326"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-326" id="mwCcM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCcQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcU">[</span>326<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcY">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="41" id="mwCcc"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Distributing_emissions_abatement_costs">Distributing emissions abatement costs</h3>
<p id="mwCcg">Mitigation at the speed and scale required to limit warming to 2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCck"> </span>°C or below implies deep economic and structural changes. These raise multiple types of distributional concerns across regions, income classes and sectors.<sup about="#mwt1217" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_3_322-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 3"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_3-322" id="mwCco"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCcs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCcw">[</span>322<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCc0">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCc4">There have been different proposals on how to allocate responsibility for cutting emissions.<sup about="#mwt1222" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-banuri_327-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"banuri"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-banuri-327"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-banuri-327" id="mwCc8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCdA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdE">[</span>327<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdI">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1218" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"103"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCdM"><span title="Page / location: 103"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>103<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> These include <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Egalitarianism" title="Egalitarianism" id="mwCdQ">egalitarianism</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Basic_needs" title="Basic needs" id="mwCdU">basic needs</a> according to a minimum level of consumption, proportionality and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Polluter_pays_principle" title="Polluter pays principle" id="mwCdY">polluter-pays principle</a>. A specific proposal is "equal per capita entitlements".<sup about="#mwt1223" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-banuri_327-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"banuri"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-banuri-327" id="mwCdc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCdg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdk">[</span>327<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCdo">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1219" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"106"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCds"><span title="Page / location: 106"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>106<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> This approach has two categories. In the first category, emissions are allocated according to national population. In the second category, emissions are allocated in a way that attempts to account for historical or cumulative emissions.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="42" id="mwCdw"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Funding">Funding</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1224" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Climate finance"},"2":{"wt":"Economics of climate change mitigation#Finance"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCd0"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1225" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1224" id="mwCd4">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_finance" title="Climate finance">Climate finance</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Economics_of_climate_change_mitigation#Finance" title="Economics of climate change mitigation" class="mw-redirect">Economics of climate change mitigation §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Finance</a></div>

<p id="mwCd8">In order to reconcile economic development with mitigating carbon emissions, developing countries need particular support. This would be both financial and technical. The IPCC found that accelerated support would also tackle inequities in financial and economic vulnerability to climate change.<sup about="#mwt1228" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-328" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-328"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-328" id="mwCeA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCeE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCeI">[</span>328<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCeM">]</span></span></a></sup> One way to achieve this is the Kyoto Protocol's <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Clean_Development_Mechanism" title="Clean Development Mechanism" id="mwCeQ">Clean Development Mechanism</a> (CDM).</p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="43" id="mwCeU"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Policies">Policies</h2>

<section data-mw-section-id="44" id="mwCeY"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="National_policies">National policies</h3>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCec"><a href="./File:Total_CO2_emissions_by_country_in_2017_vs_per_capita_emissions_(top_40_countries).svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCeg"><img resource="./File:Total_CO2_emissions_by_country_in_2017_vs_per_capita_emissions_(top_40_countries).svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Total_CO2_emissions_by_country_in_2017_vs_per_capita_emissions_%28top_40_countries%29.svg/250px-Total_CO2_emissions_by_country_in_2017_vs_per_capita_emissions_%28top_40_countries%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e7/Total_CO2_emissions_by_country_in_2017_vs_per_capita_emissions_%28top_40_countries%29.svg/500px-Total_CO2_emissions_by_country_in_2017_vs_per_capita_emissions_%28top_40_countries%29.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="810" data-file-height="1080" data-file-type="drawing" height="333" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCek"/></a><figcaption id="mwCeo">Although China is the leading producer of <span about="#mwt1237" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCes">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt1237" id="mwCew">2</sub> emissions in the world with the U.S. second, per capita the U.S. leads China by a fair margin (data from 2017).</figcaption></figure><p id="mwCe0">Climate change mitigation policies can have a large and complex impact on the socio-economic status of individuals and countries. This can be both positive and negative.<sup about="#mwt1231" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-329" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-329"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-329" id="mwCe4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCe8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfA">[</span>329<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfE">]</span></span></a></sup> It is important to design policies well and make them inclusive. Otherwise climate change mitigation measures can impose higher financial costs on poor households.<sup about="#mwt1234" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-330" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-330"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-330" id="mwCfI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCfM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfQ">[</span>330<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfU">]</span></span></a></sup> Current climate policies contribute to some changes but remain far from accelerating low-carbon transitions beyond historical trends and to levels consistent with Paris climate targets,<sup about="#mwt1235" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Suzuki2023_5-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Suzuki2023"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Suzuki2023-5" id="mwCfY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCfc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfg">[</span>5<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCfk">]</span></span></a></sup> and would still result in global warming of about 2.7<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCfo"> </span>°C by 2100.<sup about="#mwt1236" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Ritchie2022_6-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Ritchie2022"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Ritchie2022-6" id="mwCfs"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCfw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCf0">[</span>6<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCf4">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCf8">In order to identify relatively more effective policies, an evaluation was conducted on 1,500 climate policy interventions made between 1998 and 2022.<sup about="#mwt1239" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Stechemesser2024_4-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Stechemesser2024"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Stechemesser2024-4" id="mwCgA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCgE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgI">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgM">]</span></span></a></sup> The interventions took place in 41 countries and across 6 continents, which together contributed 81% of the world's total emissions as of 2019. The evaluation found 63 successful interventions that resulted in significant emission reductions; the total <span about="#mwt1238" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCgQ">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt1238" id="mwCgU">2</sub> release averted by these interventions was between 0.6 and 1.8 billion metric tonnes. The study focused on interventions with at least 4.5% emission reductions, but the researchers noted that meeting the reductions required by the Paris Agreement would require 23 billion metric tonnes per year. Generally, carbon pricing was found to be most effective in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Developed_country" title="Developed country" id="mwCgY">developed countries</a>, while regulation was most effective in the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Developing_country" title="Developing country" id="mwCgc">developing countries</a>. Complementary policy mixes benefited from synergies, and were mostly found to be more effective interventions than the implementation of isolated policies.<sup about="#mwt1242" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-331" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-331"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-331" id="mwCgg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCgk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgo">[</span>331<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCgs">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1245" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-332" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-332"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-332" id="mwCgw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCg0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCg4">[</span>332<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCg8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1248" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-The_Boston_Globe_9-4-2024_333-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"The Boston Globe 9-4-2024"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-The_Boston_Globe_9-4-2024-333"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-The_Boston_Globe_9-4-2024-333" id="mwChA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwChE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChI">[</span>333<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChM">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwChQ">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./OECD" title="OECD" id="mwChU">OECD</a> recognise 48 distinct climate mitigation policies suitable for implementation at national level. Broadly, these can be categorised into three types: <i id="mwChY">market based</i> instruments, <i id="mwChc">non market based</i> instruments and <i id="mwChg">other</i> policies.<sup about="#mwt1251" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-capmf_334-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"capmf"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-capmf-334"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-capmf-334" id="mwChk"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCho"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChs">[</span>334<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwChw">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1252" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Stechemesser2024_4-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Stechemesser2024"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Stechemesser2024-4" id="mwCh0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCh4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCh8">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCiA">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<ul id="mwCiE"><li id="mwCiI"><b id="mwCiM">Other</b> policies include the <i id="mwCiQ">Establishing an Independent climate advisory body</i>.<sup about="#mwt1253" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-capmf_334-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"capmf"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-capmf-334" id="mwCiU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCiY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCic">[</span>334<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCig">]</span></span></a></sup></li>
<li id="mwCik"><b id="mwCio">Non market based</b> policies include the Implementing or tightening of <i id="mwCis">Regulatory standards</i>. These set technology or performance standards. They can be effective in addressing the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Market_failure" title="Market failure" id="mwCiw">market failure</a> of informational barriers.<sup about="#mwt1257" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bashmakov3_335-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bashmakov3"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-bashmakov3-335"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-bashmakov3-335" id="mwCi0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCi4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCi8">[</span>335<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1254" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"412"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCjE"><span title="Page / location: 412"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>412<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></li>
<li id="mwCjI">Among <b id="mwCjM">market based</b> policies, the <i id="mwCjQ">carbon price</i> has been found to be the most effective (at least for developed economies),<sup about="#mwt1258" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Stechemesser2024_4-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Stechemesser2024"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Stechemesser2024-4" id="mwCjU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCjY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjc">[</span>4<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCjg">]</span></span></a></sup> and has its own section below. Additional <i id="mwCjk">market based</i> policy instruments for climate change mitigation include:</li></ul>
<p id="mwCjo"><i id="mwCjs">Emissions taxes</i> These often require domestic emitters to pay a fixed fee or tax for every tonne of CO<sub id="mwCjw">2</sub> emissions they release into the atmosphere.<sup about="#mwt1260" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bashmakov3_335-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bashmakov3"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-bashmakov3-335" id="mwCj0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCj4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCj8">[</span>335<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1259" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"4123"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCkE"><span title="Page / location: 4123"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>4123<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Methane_emissions" title="Methane emissions" id="mwCkI">Methane emissions</a> from fossil fuel extraction are also occasionally taxed.<sup about="#mwt1263" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-336" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-336"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-336" id="mwCkM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCkQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkU">[</span>336<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkY">]</span></span></a></sup> But methane and nitrous oxide from agriculture are typically not subject to tax.<sup about="#mwt1266" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-337" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-337"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-337" id="mwCkc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCkg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCkk">[</span>337<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCko">]</span></span></a></sup>
<br id="mwCks"/><i id="mwCkw">Removing unhelpful subsidies:</i> Many countries provide subsidies for activities that affect emissions. For example, significant <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Fossil_fuel_subsidies" title="Fossil fuel subsidies" id="mwCk0">fossil fuel subsidies</a> are present in many countries.<sup about="#mwt1269" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-338" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-338"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-338" id="mwCk4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCk8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClA">[</span>338<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClE">]</span></span></a></sup> <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Fossil_fuel_phase-out#Phase-out_of_fossil_fuel_subsidies" title="Fossil fuel phase-out" id="mwClI">Phasing-out fossil fuel subsidies</a> is crucial to address the climate crisis.<sup about="#mwt1272" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-339" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-339"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-339" id="mwClM"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClQ"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClU">[</span>339<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClY">]</span></span></a></sup> It must however be done carefully to avoid protests<sup about="#mwt1275" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-340" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-340"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-340" id="mwClc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClk">[</span>340<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwClo">]</span></span></a></sup> and making poor people poorer.<sup about="#mwt1278" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-341" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-341"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-341" id="mwCls"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwClw"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCl0">[</span>341<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCl4">]</span></span></a></sup>
<br id="mwCl8"/><i id="mwCmA">Creating helpful subsidies</i>: Creating subsidies and financial incentives.<sup about="#mwt1281" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-342" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-342"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-342" id="mwCmE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmM">[</span>342<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmQ">]</span></span></a></sup> One example is <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Energy_subsidy" title="Energy subsidy" id="mwCmU">energy subsidies</a> to support clean generation which is not yet commercially viable such as tidal power.<sup about="#mwt1284" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-343" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-343"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-343" id="mwCmY"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCmc"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmg">[</span>343<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCmk">]</span></span></a></sup>
<br id="mwCmo"/><i id="mwCms">Tradable permits</i>: A <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_emission_trading" title="Carbon emission trading" id="mwCmw">permit system</a> can limit emissions.<sup about="#mwt1286" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bashmakov3_335-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bashmakov3"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-bashmakov3-335" id="mwCm0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCm4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCm8">[</span>335<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCnA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1285" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"415"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCnE"><span title="Page / location: 415"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>415<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="45" id="mwCnI"><h4 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Carbon_pricing">Carbon pricing</h4>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1287" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Carbon price"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCnM"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1288" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1287" id="mwCnQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_price" title="Carbon price">Carbon price</a></div>

<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCnU"><a href="./File:ETS-allowance-prices.svg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCnY"><img resource="./File:ETS-allowance-prices.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/ETS-allowance-prices.svg/500px-ETS-allowance-prices.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/ETS-allowance-prices.svg/960px-ETS-allowance-prices.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="768" data-file-height="576" data-file-type="drawing" height="255" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwCnc"/></a><figcaption id="mwCng">Carbon emission trade – allowance prices from 2008</figcaption></figure>
<p id="mwCnk">Imposing additional costs on greenhouse gas emissions can make fossil fuels less competitive and accelerate investments into low-carbon sources of energy. A growing number of countries raise a fixed <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_tax" title="Carbon tax" id="mwCno">carbon tax</a> or participate in dynamic <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_emission_trading" title="Carbon emission trading" id="mwCns">carbon emission trading</a> (ETS) systems. In 2021, more than 21% of global greenhouse gas emissions were covered by a carbon price. This was a big increase from earlier due to the introduction of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Chinese_national_carbon_trading_scheme" title="Chinese national carbon trading scheme" id="mwCnw">Chinese national carbon trading scheme</a>.<sup about="#mwt1293" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-344" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-344"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-344" id="mwCn0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCn4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCn8">[</span>344<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoA">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1289" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"23"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCoE"><span title="Page / location: 23"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>23<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwCoI">Trading schemes offer the possibility to limit emission allowances to certain reduction targets. However, an oversupply of allowances keeps most ETS at low price levels around $10 with a low impact. This includes the Chinese ETS which started with $7/t<span about="#mwt1294" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCoM">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt1294" id="mwCoQ">2</sub> in 2021.<sup about="#mwt1299" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-345" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-345"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-345" id="mwCoU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCoY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCoc">[</span>345<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCog">]</span></span></a></sup> One exception is the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./European_Union_Emission_Trading_Scheme" title="European Union Emission Trading Scheme" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCok">European Union Emission Trading Scheme</a> where prices began to rise in 2018. They reached about €80/t<span about="#mwt1295" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCoo">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt1295" id="mwCos">2</sub> in 2022.<sup about="#mwt1302" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-346" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-346"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-346" id="mwCow"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCo0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCo4">[</span>346<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCo8">]</span></span></a></sup> This results in additional costs of about €0.04/KWh for coal and €0.02/KWh for gas combustion for electricity, depending on the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Emission_intensity" title="Emission intensity" id="mwCpA">emission intensity</a>.<sup about="#mwt1303" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-347" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-347"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-347" id="mwCpE"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCpI"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpM">[</span>347<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpQ">]</span></span></a></sup> Industries which have high energy requirements and high emissions often pay only very low energy taxes, or even none at all.<sup about="#mwt1304" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_11_348-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 11"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_11-348"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_11-348" id="mwCpU"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCpY"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpc">[</span>348<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCpg">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1296" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"11–80"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCpk"><span title="Page / location: 11–80"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>11–80<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwCpo">While this is often part of national schemes, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_offsets_and_credits" title="Carbon offsets and credits" id="mwCps">carbon offsets and credits</a> can be part of a voluntary market as well such as on the international market. Notably, the company <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Blue_Carbon_(company)" title="Blue Carbon (company)" id="mwCpw">Blue Carbon</a> of the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./UAE" title="UAE" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCp0">UAE</a> has bought ownership over an area equivalent to the United Kingdom to be preserved in return for carbon credits.<sup about="#mwt1307" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-349" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-349"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-349" id="mwCp4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCp8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqA">[</span>349<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="46" id="mwCqI"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="International_agreements">International agreements</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1308" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Politics of climate change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCqM"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1309" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1308" id="mwCqQ">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Politics_of_climate_change" title="Politics of climate change">Politics of climate change</a></div>

<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1310" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Climate change#Policies and politics"},"2":{"wt":"Climate change mitigation framework"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCqU"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1311" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1310" id="mwCqY">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change#Policies_and_politics" title="Climate change">Climate change §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Policies and politics</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_mitigation_framework" title="Climate change mitigation framework">Climate change mitigation framework</a></div>

<p id="mwCqc"><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./International_cooperation" title="International cooperation" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCqg">International cooperation</a> is considered a <i id="mwCqk">critical enabler</i> for climate action<sup about="#mwt1313" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_13_11-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 13"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_13-11" id="mwCqo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCqs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCqw">[</span>11<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCq0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1312" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"52"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCq4"><span title="Page / location: 52"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>52<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> while conflicts generally hamper it.<sup about="#mwt1316" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-350" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-350"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-350" id="mwCq8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCrA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrE">[</span>350<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrI">]</span></span></a></sup> Almost all countries are parties to the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./United_Nations_Framework_Convention_on_Climate_Change" title="United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change" id="mwCrM">United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change</a> (UNFCCC).<sup about="#mwt1319" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-351" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-351"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-351" id="mwCrQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCrU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrY">[</span>351<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrc">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1322" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-352" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-352"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-352" id="mwCrg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCrk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCro">[</span>352<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCrs">]</span></span></a></sup> The ultimate objective of the UNFCCC is to stabilise atmospheric concentrations of greenhouse gases at a level that would prevent dangerous human interference with the climate system.<sup about="#mwt1325" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-353" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-353"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-353" id="mwCrw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCr0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCr4">[</span>353<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCr8">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCsA">Although not designed for this purpose, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Montreal_Protocol" title="Montreal Protocol" id="mwCsE">Montreal Protocol</a> has benefited climate change mitigation efforts.<sup about="#mwt1328" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-354" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-354"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-354" id="mwCsI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCsM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsQ">[</span>354<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsU">]</span></span></a></sup> The Montreal Protocol is an international treaty that has successfully reduced emissions of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Ozone-depleting_substance" title="Ozone-depleting substance" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCsY">ozone-depleting substances</a> such as <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Chlorofluorocarbon" title="Chlorofluorocarbon" id="mwCsc">CFCs</a>. These are also greenhouse gases.</p>

<section data-mw-section-id="47" id="mwCsg"><h4 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Supply-side_vs._demand-side_policies">Supply-side vs. demand-side policies</h4>
<p id="mwCsk">While international climate efforts have traditionally focused on demand-side policies like carbon taxes and emissions trading, a growing body of research explores supply-side treaties aimed at reducing the global output of fossil fuels. Proponents argue these treaties may be more cost-efficient and easier to monitor than demand-side agreements, potentially addressing issues like 'carbon leakage' and the 'green paradox'.<sup about="#mwt1331" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Stankovic-2024_355-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Stankovic-2024"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Stankovic-2024-355"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Stankovic-2024-355" id="mwCso"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCss"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCsw">[</span>355<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCs0">]</span></span></a></sup> However, scholars note that because most major emitters are also major producers, supply-side treaties face similar challenges in participation and distributive conflict.<sup about="#mwt1332" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Stankovic-2024_355-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Stankovic-2024"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Stankovic-2024-355" id="mwCs4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCs8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtA">[</span>355<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section></section><section data-mw-section-id="48" id="mwCtI"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="History">History</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1333" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Climate change mitigation framework"},"2":{"wt":"History of climate change policy and politics"},"3":{"wt":"Kyoto Protocol#Chronology"},"4":{"wt":"Paris Agreement#Development"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCtM"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1334" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1333" id="mwCtQ">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_mitigation_framework" title="Climate change mitigation framework">Climate change mitigation framework</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./History_of_climate_change_policy_and_politics" title="History of climate change policy and politics">History of climate change policy and politics</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Kyoto_Protocol#Chronology" title="Kyoto Protocol">Kyoto Protocol §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Chronology</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Paris_Agreement#Development" title="Paris Agreement">Paris Agreement §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Development</a></div>
<p id="mwCtU">Historically efforts to deal with climate change have taken place at a multinational level. They involve attempts to reach a consensus decision at the United Nations, under the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./United_Nations_Framework_Convention_on_Climate_Change" title="United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change" id="mwCtY">United Nations Framework Convention on Climate Change</a> (UNFCCC).<sup about="#mwt1337" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-356" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-356"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-356" id="mwCtc"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCtg"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCtk">[</span>356<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCto">]</span></span></a></sup> This is the dominant approach historically of engaging as many international governments as possible in taking action on a worldwide public issue. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Montreal_Protocol" title="Montreal Protocol" id="mwCts">Montreal Protocol</a> in 1987 is a precedent that this approach can work. But some critics say the top-down framework of only utilising the UNFCCC consensus approach is ineffective. They put forward counter-proposals of bottom-up governance. At this same time this would lessen the emphasis on the UNFCCC.<sup about="#mwt1340" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Cole-2015_357-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Cole-2015"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Cole-2015-357"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Cole-2015-357" id="mwCtw"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCt0"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCt4">[</span>357<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCt8">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1343" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Sabel-2017_358-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Sabel-2017"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Sabel-2017-358"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Sabel-2017-358" id="mwCuA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCuE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuI">[</span>358<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1346" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Zefferman-2018_359-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Zefferman-2018"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Zefferman-2018-359"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Zefferman-2018-359" id="mwCuQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCuU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuY">[</span>359<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwCug">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Kyoto_Protocol" title="Kyoto Protocol" id="mwCuk">Kyoto Protocol</a> to the UNFCCC adopted in 1997 set out legally binding emission reduction commitments for the "Annex 1" countries.<sup about="#mwt1352" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-360" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-360"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-360" id="mwCuo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCus"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCuw">[</span>360<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCu0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1347" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"817"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCu4"><span title="Page / location: 817"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>817<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> The Protocol defined three international policy instruments ("<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Flexibility_mechanisms" title="Flexibility mechanisms" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCu8">Flexibility Mechanisms</a>") which could be used by the Annex 1 countries to meet their emission reduction commitments. According to Bashmakov, use of these instruments could significantly reduce the costs for Annex 1 countries in meeting their emission reduction commitments.<sup about="#mwt1355" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-bashmakov_361-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"bashmakov"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-bashmakov-361"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-bashmakov-361" id="mwCvA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCvE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvI">[</span>361<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCvM">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1348" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"402"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCvQ"><span title="Page / location: 402"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>402<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup><sup class="noprint Inline-Template " style="white-space:nowrap;" about="#mwt1349" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Update inline","href":"./Template:Update_inline"},"params":{"date":{"wt":"January 2022"},"reason":{"wt":"were they used and did they work?"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCvU"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span><i><a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Wikipedia:Manual_of_Style/Dates_and_numbers#Chronological_items" title="Wikipedia:Manual of Style/Dates and numbers"><span title="were they used and did they work? (January 2022)">needs update</span></a></i><span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></sup><span typeof="mw:Nowiki" about="#mwt1349"></span><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Wikipedia_articles_in_need_of_updating_from_January_2022" about="#mwt1349"/><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:All_Wikipedia_articles_in_need_of_updating" about="#mwt1349" id="mwCvY"/></p>

<p id="mwCvc">The Paris Agreement reached in 2015 succeeded the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Kyoto_Protocol" title="Kyoto Protocol" id="mwCvg">Kyoto Protocol</a> which expired in 2020. <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./List_of_Kyoto_Protocol_signatories" title="List of Kyoto Protocol signatories" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCvk">Countries that ratified the Kyoto protocol</a> committed to reduce their emissions of carbon dioxide and five other greenhouse gases, or engage in <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_emissions_trading" title="Carbon emissions trading" class="mw-redirect" id="mwCvo">carbon emissions trading</a> if they maintain or increase emissions of these gases.</p>

<p id="mwCvs">In 2015, the UNFCCC's "structured expert dialogue" came to the conclusion that, "in some regions and vulnerable ecosystems, high risks are projected even for warming above 1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCvw"> </span>°C".<sup about="#mwt1358" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-362" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-362"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-362" id="mwCv0"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCv4"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCv8">[</span>362<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwA">]</span></span></a></sup> Together with the strong diplomatic voice of the poorest countries and the island nations in the Pacific, this expert finding was the driving force leading to the decision of the 2015 <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./2015_United_Nations_Climate_Change_Conference" title="2015 United Nations Climate Change Conference" id="mwCwE">Paris Climate Conference</a> to lay down this 1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCwI"> </span>°C long-term target on top of the existing 2<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwCwM"> </span>°C goal.<sup about="#mwt1361" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-363" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-363"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-363" id="mwCwQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCwU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwY">[</span>363<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCwc">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="49" id="mwCwg"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Barriers">Barriers</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1362" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"See also","href":"./Template:See_also"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Economic analysis of climate change#Economic barriers to addressing climate change mitigation"},"2":{"wt":"Climate change denial"},"3":{"wt":"Public opinion on climate change"},"4":{"wt":"Sustainability#Barriers"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCwk"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1363" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1362" id="mwCwo">See also: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Economic_analysis_of_climate_change#Economic_barriers_to_addressing_climate_change_mitigation" title="Economic analysis of climate change">Economic analysis of climate change §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Economic barriers to addressing climate change mitigation</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_denial" title="Climate change denial">Climate change denial</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Public_opinion_on_climate_change" title="Public opinion on climate change">Public opinion on climate change</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Sustainability#Barriers" title="Sustainability">Sustainability §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Barriers</a></div>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCws"><a href="./File:A_typology_of_climate_delay_discourses.png" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCww"><img resource="./File:A_typology_of_climate_delay_discourses.png" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/A_typology_of_climate_delay_discourses.png/250px-A_typology_of_climate_delay_discourses.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/A_typology_of_climate_delay_discourses.png/500px-A_typology_of_climate_delay_discourses.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="2000" data-file-height="1642" data-file-type="bitmap" height="205" width="250" class="mw-file-element" id="mwCw0"/></a><figcaption id="mwCw4">A typology of discourses aimed at delaying climate change mitigation<sup about="#mwt1364" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Lamb_et_al._307-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Lamb et al."}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Lamb_et_al.-307" id="mwCw8"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCxA"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxE">[</span>307<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxI">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwCxM"><a href="./File:Distribution_of_committed_CO2_emissions_from_developed_fossil_fuel_reserves.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwCxQ"><img resource="./File:Distribution_of_committed_CO2_emissions_from_developed_fossil_fuel_reserves.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Distribution_of_committed_CO2_emissions_from_developed_fossil_fuel_reserves.jpg/500px-Distribution_of_committed_CO2_emissions_from_developed_fossil_fuel_reserves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d4/Distribution_of_committed_CO2_emissions_from_developed_fossil_fuel_reserves.jpg/960px-Distribution_of_committed_CO2_emissions_from_developed_fossil_fuel_reserves.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1725" data-file-height="2176" data-file-type="bitmap" height="429" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwCxU"/></a><figcaption id="mwCxY">Distribution of committed <span about="#mwt1365" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"CO2","href":"./Template:CO2"},"params":{},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCxc">CO</span><sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em" about="#mwt1365" id="mwCxg">2</sub> emissions from developed fossil fuel reserves</figcaption></figure>

<p id="mwCxk">There are individual, institutional and market barriers to achieving climate change mitigation.<sup about="#mwt1367" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_5_101-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 5"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_5-101" id="mwCxo"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCxs"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCxw">[</span>101<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCx0">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1366" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"5–71"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCx4"><span title="Page / location: 5–71"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>5–71<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> They differ for all the different mitigation options, regions and societies.</p>

<p id="mwCx8">Difficulties with <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Carbon_accounting" title="Carbon accounting" id="mwCyA">accounting for carbon dioxide removal</a> can act as economic barriers. This would apply to BECCS (<a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Bioenergy_with_carbon_capture_and_storage" title="Bioenergy with carbon capture and storage" id="mwCyE">bioenergy with carbon capture and storage</a>).<sup about="#mwt1369" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_6_49-4" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 6"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_6-49" id="mwCyI"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCyM"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyQ">[</span>49<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyU">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1368" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"6–42"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCyY"><span title="Page / location: 6–42"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>6–42<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwCyc">For land-based mitigation options, finance is a major barrier. Other barriers are cultural values, governance, accountability and institutional capacity.<sup about="#mwt1371" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_7_132-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 7"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_7-132" id="mwCyg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCyk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCyo">[</span>132<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCys">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1370" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"7-5"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwCyw"><span title="Page / location: 7–5"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>7–5<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></p>

<p id="mwCy0">Developing countries face further barriers to mitigation:<sup about="#mwt1374" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-364" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-364"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-364" id="mwCy4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCy8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzA">[</span>364<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<ul id="mwCzI"><li id="mwCzM">The cost of capital increased in the early 2020s.<sup about="#mwt1377" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-365" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-365"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-365" id="mwCzQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCzU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzY">[</span>365<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzc">]</span></span></a></sup> A lack of available capital and finance is common in developing countries.<sup about="#mwt1380" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-366" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-366"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-366" id="mwCzg"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCzk"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzo">[</span>366<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwCzs">]</span></span></a></sup> Together with the absence of regulatory standards, this barrier supports the proliferation of inefficient equipment.</li>
<li id="mwCzw">There are also financial and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Capacity_building" title="Capacity building" id="mwCz0">capacity</a> barrier in many of these countries.<sup about="#mwt1382" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_5_101-6" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 5"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_5-101" id="mwCz4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwCz8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0A">[</span>101<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0E">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1381" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"97"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC0I"><span title="Page / location: 97"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>97<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup></li></ul>

<p id="mwC0M">One study estimates that only 0.12% of all funding for climate-related research goes on the social science of climate change mitigation.<sup about="#mwt1385" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Overland-2020_367-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Overland-2020"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Overland-2020-367"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Overland-2020-367" id="mwC0Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC0U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0Y">[</span>367<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0c">]</span></span></a></sup> Vastly more funding goes on natural science studies of climate change. Considerable sums also go on studies of the impact of climate change and adaptation to it.<sup about="#mwt1386" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Overland-2020_367-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Overland-2020"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Overland-2020-367" id="mwC0g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC0k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0o">[</span>367<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC0s">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="50" id="mwC0w"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Society_and_culture">Society and culture</h2>

<section data-mw-section-id="51" id="mwC00"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Commitments_to_divest">Commitments to divest</h3>
<figure class="mw-default-size" typeof="mw:File/Thumb" id="mwC04"><a href="./File:Climate_investment_is_stalling,_but_more_firms_plan_to_invest,_with_firms_in_low-carbon_sectors_taking_the_lead.jpg" class="mw-file-description" id="mwC08"><img resource="./File:Climate_investment_is_stalling,_but_more_firms_plan_to_invest,_with_firms_in_low-carbon_sectors_taking_the_lead.jpg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Climate_investment_is_stalling%2C_but_more_firms_plan_to_invest%2C_with_firms_in_low-carbon_sectors_taking_the_lead.jpg/500px-Climate_investment_is_stalling%2C_but_more_firms_plan_to_invest%2C_with_firms_in_low-carbon_sectors_taking_the_lead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3b/Climate_investment_is_stalling%2C_but_more_firms_plan_to_invest%2C_with_firms_in_low-carbon_sectors_taking_the_lead.jpg/960px-Climate_investment_is_stalling%2C_but_more_firms_plan_to_invest%2C_with_firms_in_low-carbon_sectors_taking_the_lead.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="4626" data-file-height="2576" data-file-type="bitmap" height="189" width="340" class="mw-file-element mw-file-upright" style="--mw-file-upright: 1.35" id="mwC1A"/></a><figcaption id="mwC1E">More firms plan to invest in climate change mitigation, specifically focusing on low-carbon sectors.<sup about="#mwt1395" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-368" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-368"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-368" id="mwC1I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC1M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1Q">[</span>368<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1U">]</span></span></a></sup></figcaption></figure><p id="mwC1Y">More than 1000 organisations with investments worth US$8 trillion have made commitments to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Fossil_fuel_divestment" title="Fossil fuel divestment" id="mwC1c">fossil fuel divestment</a>.<sup about="#mwt1389" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-369" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-369"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-369" id="mwC1g"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC1k"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1o">[</span>369<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC1s">]</span></span></a></sup> Socially responsible investing funds allow investors to invest in funds that meet high <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Environmental,_social_and_corporate_governance" title="Environmental, social and corporate governance" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC1w">environmental, social and corporate governance</a> (ESG) standards.<sup about="#mwt1392" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-370" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-370"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-370" id="mwC10"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC14"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC18">[</span>370<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2A">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="52" id="mwC2E"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Impacts_of_the_COVID-19_pandemic">Impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1396" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the environment#Climate change"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC2I"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1397" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1396" id="mwC2M">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Impact_of_the_COVID-19_pandemic_on_the_environment#Climate_change" title="Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the environment">Impact of the COVID-19 pandemic on the environment §<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>Climate change</a></div>

<p id="mwC2Q">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./COVID-19_pandemic" title="COVID-19 pandemic" id="mwC2U">COVID-19 pandemic</a> led some governments to shift their focus away from climate action, at least temporarily.<sup about="#mwt1400" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-371" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-371"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-371" id="mwC2Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC2c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2g">[</span>371<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2k">]</span></span></a></sup> This obstacle to environmental policy efforts may have contributed to slowed investment in green energy technologies. The economic slowdown resulting from COVID-19 added to this effect.<sup about="#mwt1403" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-UN_372-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"UN"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-UN-372"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-UN-372" id="mwC2o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC2s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC2w">[</span>372<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC20">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1406" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-9mMFi_373-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"9mMFi"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-9mMFi-373"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-9mMFi-373" id="mwC24"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC28"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3A">[</span>373<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3E">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC3I">In 2020, carbon dioxide emissions fell by 6.4% or 2.3 billion tonnes globally.<sup about="#mwt1409" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tollefson-2021_374-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tollefson-2021"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Tollefson-2021-374"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Tollefson-2021-374" id="mwC3M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC3Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3U">[</span>374<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3Y">]</span></span></a></sup> Greenhouse gas emissions rebounded later in the pandemic as many countries began lifting restrictions. The direct impact of pandemic policies had a negligible long-term impact on climate change.<sup about="#mwt1410" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Tollefson-2021_374-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Tollefson-2021"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Tollefson-2021-374" id="mwC3c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC3g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3k">[</span>374<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC3o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1413" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-effectspaper_375-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"effectspaper"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-effectspaper-375"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-effectspaper-375" id="mwC3s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC3w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC30">[</span>375<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC34">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="53" id="mwC38"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Examples_by_country">Examples by country</h2>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1414" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"multiple image\n","href":"./Template:Multiple_image"},"params":{"total_width":{"wt":"450"},"image1":{"wt":"20210626 Variwide chart of greenhouse gas emissions per capita by country.svg"},"caption1":{"wt":"Greenhouse gas emissions &apos;&apos;per person&apos;&apos; in the highest-emitting countries.&lt;ref name=GlobalCarbonAtlas_Territorial_MtCO2>● Source for carbon emissions data: {{cite web |title=Territorial (MtCO&lt;sub>2&lt;/sub>) / Emissions / Carbon emissions / Chart View |url=https://globalcarbonatlas.org/emissions/carbon-emissions/ |date=2024 |publisher=Global Carbon Atlas }}&lt;br />● Source for country population data: {{cite web |title=Population 2022 |url=https://databankfiles.worldbank.org/public/ddpext_download/POP.pdf |publisher=World Bank |archive-url=https://archive.today/20241022200208/https://databankfiles.worldbank.org/public/ddpext_download/POP.pdf |archive-date=22 October 2024 |date=2024 |url-status=live }}&lt;/ref> Though China has the greatest total annual carbon dioxide emissions, the U.S. and a few other high-emitting countries exceed China in &apos;&apos;per capita&apos;&apos; emissions."},"image2":{"wt":"2021 Carbon dioxide (CO2) emissions per person versus GDP per person - scatter plot.svg"},"caption2":{"wt":"Richer [[Developed country|(developed)]] countries emit more {{CO2}} per person than poorer [[Developing country|(developing)]] countries.&lt;ref name=WashPost_20230301>{{cite news |last1=Stevens |first1=Harry |title=The United States has caused the most global warming. When will China pass it? |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/interactive/2023/global-warming-carbon-emissions-china-us/ |newspaper=The Washington Post |date=1 March 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230301130719/https://www.washingtonpost.com/climate-environment/interactive/2023/global-warming-carbon-emissions-china-us/ |archive-date=1 March 2023 |url-status=live }}&lt;/ref> Emissions are roughly proportional to [[Gross domestic product|GDP]] per person, though the rate of increase diminishes with average GDP/pp of about $10,000."}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC4A"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1349637415/mw-parser-output/.tmulti" about="#mwt1415" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Multiple image/styles.css","wrapper":".tmulti"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div class="thumb tmulti floatright" about="#mwt1414"><div class="thumbinner multiimageinner" style="width:442px;max-width:442px"><div class="trow"><div class="tsingle" style="width:250px;max-width:250px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:139px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:20210626_Variwide_chart_of_greenhouse_gas_emissions_per_capita_by_country.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:20210626_Variwide_chart_of_greenhouse_gas_emissions_per_capita_by_country.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/20210626_Variwide_chart_of_greenhouse_gas_emissions_per_capita_by_country.svg/250px-20210626_Variwide_chart_of_greenhouse_gas_emissions_per_capita_by_country.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/20210626_Variwide_chart_of_greenhouse_gas_emissions_per_capita_by_country.svg/500px-20210626_Variwide_chart_of_greenhouse_gas_emissions_per_capita_by_country.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1600" data-file-height="900" data-file-type="drawing" height="140" width="248" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">Greenhouse gas emissions <i>per person</i> in the highest-emitting countries.<sup about="#mwt1421" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-GlobalCarbonAtlas_Territorial_MtCO2_376-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"GlobalCarbonAtlas_Territorial_MtCO2"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-GlobalCarbonAtlas_Territorial_MtCO2-376"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-GlobalCarbonAtlas_Territorial_MtCO2-376" id="mwC4E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC4I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4M">[</span>376<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4Q">]</span></span></a></sup> Though China has the greatest total annual carbon dioxide emissions, the U.S. and a few other high-emitting countries exceed China in <i>per capita</i> emissions.</div></div><div class="tsingle" style="width:188px;max-width:188px"><div class="thumbimage" style="height:139px;overflow:hidden"><span typeof="mw:File"><a href="./File:2021_Carbon_dioxide_(CO2)_emissions_per_person_versus_GDP_per_person_-_scatter_plot.svg" class="mw-file-description"><img resource="./File:2021_Carbon_dioxide_(CO2)_emissions_per_person_versus_GDP_per_person_-_scatter_plot.svg" src="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/2021_Carbon_dioxide_%28CO2%29_emissions_per_person_versus_GDP_per_person_-_scatter_plot.svg/250px-2021_Carbon_dioxide_%28CO2%29_emissions_per_person_versus_GDP_per_person_-_scatter_plot.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="//thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/2021_Carbon_dioxide_%28CO2%29_emissions_per_person_versus_GDP_per_person_-_scatter_plot.svg/500px-2021_Carbon_dioxide_%28CO2%29_emissions_per_person_versus_GDP_per_person_-_scatter_plot.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&amp;utm_campaign=parser&amp;utm_content=thumbnail 2x" data-file-width="1000" data-file-height="750" data-file-type="drawing" height="140" width="186" class="mw-file-element"/></a></span></div><div class="thumbcaption">Richer <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Developed_country" title="Developed country">(developed)</a> countries emit more CO<sub style="font-size: 80%;vertical-align: -0.35em">2</sub> per person than poorer <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Developing_country" title="Developing country">(developing)</a> countries.<sup about="#mwt1427" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-WashPost_20230301_377-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"WashPost_20230301"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-WashPost_20230301-377"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-WashPost_20230301-377" id="mwC4U"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC4Y"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4c">[</span>377<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4g">]</span></span></a></sup> Emissions are roughly proportional to <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Gross_domestic_product" title="Gross domestic product">GDP</a> per person, though the rate of increase diminishes with average GDP/pp of about $10,000.</div></div></div></div></div><span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1414"><link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Pages_using_multiple_image_with_auto_scaled_images"/></span>

<p id="mwC4k">While climate mitigation is often cast as a burden to be distributed in some or other way, it can also be seen as an opportunity for industrial development, exports, and status seeking for countries.<sup about="#mwt1430" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Overland-2024_19-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Overland-2024"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Overland-2024-19" id="mwC4o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC4s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC4w">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC40">]</span></span></a></sup> The examples and precedents that countries set may influence other countries to follow suit.<sup about="#mwt1431" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Overland-2024_19-3" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Overland-2024"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Overland-2024-19" id="mwC44"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC48"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5A">[</span>19<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5E">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<section data-mw-section-id="54" id="mwC5I"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="United_States">United States</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1432" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"Main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Climate change in the United States"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC5M"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1433" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1432" id="mwC5Q">Main article: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_in_the_United_States" title="Climate change in the United States">Climate change in the United States</a></div>

<div class="excerpt-block" about="#mwt1434" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"excerpt","href":"./Template:Excerpt"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Greenhouse gas emissions by the United States#Federal Policies"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC5U"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1353838603" about="#mwt1435" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Excerpt/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1436" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable dablink excerpt-hat selfref">This section is an excerpt from <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenhouse_gas_emissions_by_the_United_States#Federal_Policies" title="Greenhouse gas emissions by the United States">Greenhouse gas emissions by the United States § Federal Policies</a>.<span class="mw-editsection-like plainlinks"><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">[</span><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Greenhouse_gas_emissions_by_the_United_States&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Greenhouse_gas_emissions_by_the_United_States&amp;action=edit">edit</a><span class="mw-editsection-bracket">]</span></span></div><div class="excerpt">
<p>The United States government has held shifting attitudes toward addressing greenhouse gas emissions. The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Presidency_of_George_W._Bush" title="Presidency of George W. Bush">George W. Bush administration</a> opted not to sign the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Kyoto_Protocol" title="Kyoto Protocol">Kyoto Protocol</a>,<sup about="#mwt1437" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Greenhouse_gas_emissions_by_the_United_States_Dessai_2001_5_378-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Greenhouse gas emissions by the United States Dessai 2001 5"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-Greenhouse_gas_emissions_by_the_United_States_Dessai_2001_5-378"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-Greenhouse_gas_emissions_by_the_United_States_Dessai_2001_5-378" id="mwC5Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC5c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5g">[</span>378<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5k">]</span></span></a></sup> but the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Obama_administration" title="Obama administration" class="mw-redirect">Obama administration</a> entered the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Paris_Agreement" title="Paris Agreement">Paris Agreement</a>.<sup about="#mwt1441" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-379" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-379"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-379" id="mwC5o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC5s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC5w">[</span>379<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC50">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./First_presidency_of_Donald_Trump" title="First presidency of Donald Trump">Trump administration</a> withdrew from the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Paris_Agreement" title="Paris Agreement">Paris Agreement</a> while increasing the export of <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Crude_oil" title="Crude oil" class="mw-redirect">crude oil</a> and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Gasoline" title="Gasoline">gas</a>, making the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./United_States" title="United States">United States</a> the largest producer.<sup about="#mwt1446" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-380" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-380"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-380" id="mwC54"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC58"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6A">[</span>380<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6E">]</span></span></a></sup></p> 

<p>In 2021, the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Presidency_of_Joe_Biden" title="Presidency of Joe Biden">Biden administration</a> committed to reducing emissions to half of 2005 levels by 2030.<sup about="#mwt1451" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-381" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-381"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-381" id="mwC6I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6Q">[</span>381<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6U">]</span></span></a></sup> In 2022, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Joe_Biden" title="Joe Biden">President Biden</a> signed the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Inflation_Reduction_Act_of_2022" title="Inflation Reduction Act of 2022" class="mw-redirect">Inflation Reduction Act</a> into law, which is estimated to provide around $375 billion over 10 years to fight climate change.<sup about="#mwt1456" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-382" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-382"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-382" id="mwC6Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6g">[</span>382<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6k">]</span></span></a></sup> As of 2022<sup class="plainlinks noexcerpt noprint asof-tag update" style="display:none;"><a rel="mw:ExtLink" href="https://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Climate_change_mitigation&amp;action=edit" class="external text" data-mw-original-href="//en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Climate_change_mitigation&amp;action=edit"><span typeof="mw:Entity">[</span>update<span typeof="mw:Entity">]</span></a></sup> the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Social_cost_of_carbon" title="Social cost of carbon">social cost of carbon</a> is 51 dollars a tonne whereas academics say it should be more than three times higher.<sup about="#mwt1461" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-383" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-383"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-383" id="mwC6o"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC6s"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC6w">[</span>383<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC60">]</span></span></a></sup></p>
<link rel="mw:PageProp/Category" href="./Category:Articles_with_excerpts"/>
</div></div>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="55" id="mwC64"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="China">China</h3>
<span class="mw-empty-elt" about="#mwt1464" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"main","href":"./Template:Main"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"Greenhouse gas emissions by China"},"2":{"wt":"Climate change in China"},"3":{"wt":"Debate over China&apos;s economic responsibilities for climate change mitigation"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC68"><link rel="mw-deduplicated-inline-style" href="mw-data:TemplateStyles:r1368532237" about="#mwt1465" typeof="mw:Extension/templatestyles" data-mw='{"name":"templatestyles","attrs":{"src":"Module:Hatnote/styles.css"},"body":{"extsrc":""}}'/></span><div role="note" class="hatnote navigation-not-searchable" about="#mwt1464" id="mwC7A">Main articles: <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Greenhouse_gas_emissions_by_China" title="Greenhouse gas emissions by China">Greenhouse gas emissions by China</a>, <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_change_in_China" title="Climate change in China">Climate change in China</a>, and <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Debate_over_China's_economic_responsibilities_for_climate_change_mitigation" title="Debate over China's economic responsibilities for climate change mitigation" class="mw-redirect">Debate over China's economic responsibilities for climate change mitigation</a></div>

<p id="mwC7E">China has committed to peak emissions by 2030 and reach <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Net_zero" title="Net zero" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC7I">net zero</a> by 2060.<sup about="#mwt1468" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-384" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-384"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-384" id="mwC7M"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC7Q"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7U">[</span>384<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7Y">]</span></span></a></sup> Warming cannot be limited to 1.5<span typeof="mw:Entity" id="mwC7c"> </span>°C if any <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Electricity_sector_in_China#Coal_power" title="Electricity sector in China" id="mwC7g">coal plants in China</a> (without carbon capture) operate after 2045.<sup about="#mwt1471" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-385" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-385"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-385" id="mwC7k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC7o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7s">[</span>385<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC7w">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Chinese_national_carbon_trading_scheme" title="Chinese national carbon trading scheme" id="mwC70">Chinese national carbon trading scheme</a> started in 2021.</p>

</section><section data-mw-section-id="56" id="mwC74"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="European_Union">European Union</h3>
<p id="mwC78">The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./European_Commission" title="European Commission" id="mwC8A">European Commission</a> estimates that an additional €477 million in annual investment is needed for the European Union to meet its <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Fit_for_55" title="Fit for 55" id="mwC8E">Fit-for-55</a> decarbonisation goals.<sup about="#mwt1472" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-European_Investment_Bank_285-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"European Investment Bank"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-European_Investment_Bank-285" id="mwC8I"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC8M"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8Q">[</span>285<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8U">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1475" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-386" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-386"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-386" id="mwC8Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC8c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8g">[</span>386<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC8k">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC8o">In the European Union, government-driven policies and the <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./European_Green_Deal" title="European Green Deal" id="mwC8s">European Green Deal</a> have helped position greentech (as an example) as a vital area for venture capital investment. By 2023, venture capital in the EU's greentech sector equalled that of the United States, reflecting a concerted effort to drive innovation and mitigate climate change through targeted financial support.<sup about="#mwt1476" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-European_Investment_Bank_285-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"European Investment Bank"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-European_Investment_Bank-285" id="mwC8w"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC80"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC84">[</span>285<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC88">]</span></span></a></sup><sup about="#mwt1479" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-387" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-387"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-387" id="mwC9A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC9E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9I">[</span>387<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9M">]</span></span></a></sup> The European Green Deal has fostered policies that contributed to a 30% rise in venture capital for greentech companies in the EU from 2021 to 2023, despite a downturn in other sectors during the same period.<sup about="#mwt1482" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-www.cleantechforeurope.com_388-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"www.cleantechforeurope.com"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-www.cleantechforeurope.com-388"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-www.cleantechforeurope.com-388" id="mwC9Q"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC9U"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9Y">[</span>388<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9c">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC9g">While overall venture capital investment in the EU remains about six times lower than in the United States, the greentech sector has closed this gap significantly, attracting substantial funding. Key areas benefitting from increased investments are energy storage, circular economy initiatives, and agricultural technology. This is supported by the EU's ambitious goal to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by at least 55% by 2030.<sup about="#mwt1483" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-www.cleantechforeurope.com_388-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"www.cleantechforeurope.com"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-www.cleantechforeurope.com-388" id="mwC9k"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC9o"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9s">[</span>388<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC9w">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

</section></section><section data-mw-section-id="57" id="mwC90"><h2 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Related_approaches">Related approaches</h2>

<section data-mw-section-id="58" id="mwC94"><h3 data-mw-wikitext="" id="Relationship_with_solar_radiation_modification_(SRM)"><span id="Relationship_with_solar_radiation_modification_.28SRM.29" typeof="mw:FallbackId"></span>Relationship with solar radiation modification (SRM)</h3>
<p id="mwC98">While <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Solar_geoengineering" title="Solar geoengineering" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC-A">solar radiation modification</a> (SRM) could reduce surface temperatures, it temporarily masks climate change rather than addressing the root cause, which is greenhouse gases.<sup about="#mwt1486" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_14_389-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 14"},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_14-389"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_14-389" id="mwC-E"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC-I"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-M">[</span>389<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-Q">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1484" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"14–56"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC-U"><span title="Page / location: 14–56"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>14–56<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> SRM would work by altering how much solar radiation the Earth absorbs.<sup about="#mwt1487" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_14_389-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 14"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_14-389" id="mwC-Y"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC-c"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-g">[</span>389<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-k">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1485" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"14–56"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC-o"><span title="Page / location: 14–56"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>14–56<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> Examples include reducing the amount of sunlight reaching the surface, reducing the optical thickness and lifetime of clouds, and changing the ability of the surface to reflect radiation.<sup about="#mwt1490" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-390" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{},"body":{"id":"mw-reference-text-cite_note-390"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-390" id="mwC-s"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC-w"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-0">[</span>390<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC-4">]</span></span></a></sup> The <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Intergovernmental_Panel_on_Climate_Change" title="Intergovernmental Panel on Climate Change" id="mwC-8">IPCC</a> describes SRM as a climate risk reduction strategy or supplementary option rather than a climate mitigation option.<sup about="#mwt1491" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_14_389-2" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 14"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_14-389" id="mwC_A"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_E"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_I">[</span>389<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_M">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

<p id="mwC_Q">The terminology in this area is still evolving. Experts sometimes use the term <i id="mwC_U">geoengineering</i> or <a rel="mw:WikiLink" href="./Climate_engineering" title="Climate engineering" class="mw-redirect" id="mwC_Y">climate engineering</a> in the scientific literature for both CDR or SRM, if the techniques are used at a global scale.<sup about="#mwt1493" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-AR6_WGIII_Ch_1_20-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"AR6 WGIII Ch 1"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-AR6_WGIII_Ch_1-20" id="mwC_c"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_g"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_k">[</span>20<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwC_o">]</span></span></a></sup><sup class="reference nowrap" about="#mwt1492" typeof="mw:Transclusion" data-mw='{"parts":[{"template":{"target":{"wt":"rp","href":"./Template:Rp"},"params":{"1":{"wt":"6–11"}},"i":0}}]}' id="mwC_s"><span title="Page / location: 6–11"><span typeof="mw:Entity">:</span><span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span>6–11<span typeof="mw:Entity"> </span></span></sup> IPCC reports no longer use the terms <i id="mwC_w">geoengineering</i> or <i id="mwC_0">climate engineering</i>.<sup about="#mwt1494" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-IPCC_AR6_WGI_Glossary_21-5" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"IPCC AR6 WGI Glossary"}}'><a href="./Climate_change_mitigation#cite_note-IPCC_AR6_WGI_Glossary-21" id="mwC_4"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwC_8"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAA">[</span>21<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwDAE">]</span></span></a></sup></p>

